AWS Scenario-Based Interview Q&A 

🔹 1. EC2 instance is running, but application not accessible – How to troubleshoot?

✅ Steps:

Check Security Groups → Ensure required ports (80/443) are open
Verify NACLs → Allow inbound/outbound traffic
Check Application status → systemctl status nginx / netstat -tulnp
Validate Public IP / DNS
Check OS firewall → iptables / firewalld
Review logs → /var/log/messages, app logs

💡 Example:
Port 80 was not open in Security Group → Added rule → Application became accessible.

🔹 2. Application slow during peak traffic – What to check?

✅ Check:

CloudWatch Metrics → CPU, Memory, Network
Enable Auto Scaling
Use Elastic Load Balancer (ALB)
Enable CloudFront CDN
Optimize DB (RDS read replicas)

💡 Example:
High CPU (90%) during peak → Enabled Auto Scaling → Traffic distributed → Performance improved.

🔹 3. Design a Highly Available AWS Architecture

✅ Best Practices:

Deploy across Multiple AZs
Use ALB + Auto Scaling Group
Store static content in S3
Use RDS Multi-AZ
Add Route 53 health checks

💡 Example Architecture:
Users → Route53 → ALB → EC2 (Multi-AZ) → RDS Multi-AZ

4. EC2 instance terminated accidentally – What to do?

✅ Recovery options:

Restore from AMI backup
Restore from EBS snapshot
Check Auto Scaling Group (auto-recreates instance)

💡 Example:
Instance deleted → Launched new EC2 from AMI → Attached EBS → Service restored quickly.

🔹 5. Securely access EC2 without access keys

✅ Best Methods:

Use IAM Roles (no hardcoded keys)
Use SSM Session Manager (no SSH required)
Enable MFA authentication

💡 Example:
Used SSM Session Manager → Connected to EC2 without SSH key → More secure & auditable.

🔹 6. S3 data deleted accidentally – How to recover?

✅ Recovery:

Enable Versioning → Restore previous version
Use Cross-Region Replication
Check Backup policies

💡 Example:
File deleted → Retrieved older version from S3 versioning → No data loss.
